-- The loyalty programme catalogue, as the desk settled it.
--
-- A loyalty programme is a membership, not a taste: these rows carry a client's
-- membership number and tier on `customer_preference`, so the tiers a
-- programme actually offers belong on the option beside it. The list was built
-- from a reference export and then classified by hand, which is work that
-- cannot be repeated from the deployment: there is no host shell to run the
-- importer in, and the fuzzy matching it does against what is already stored
-- would not give the same answer twice.
--
-- So the settled result is written down here instead. Keyed on (kind, slug),
-- which is the table's own unique index, so it is safe to run against a
-- database that already holds some of these.
--
-- On a row that already exists only the tier list and the grouping move. The
-- label is left alone: a programme somebody at the desk renamed keeps the name
-- they gave it, and `is_custom` keeps saying who added it. Nothing is ever
-- deleted -- a programme a client already holds a membership in must not
-- vanish underneath them.
ON CONFLICT ("kind", "slug") DO UPDATE SET
  "tiers" = excluded."tiers",
  "grouping" = excluded."grouping";
